window.docContents[213].resolve({"/Imp/#Hoare-Logic--Imp___-Simple-Imperative-Programs--Evaluating-Commands--Evaluation-as-a-Relation":{"contents":"Here's a better way: define ceval as a relation rather than a\nfunction -- i.e., make its result a Prop rather than a State,\nsimilar to what we did for Aexp.EvalR in the Slang chapter.\n\n\n\nI kind of hate this notation. Is there something more standard\nin Lean? CSLib precedent maybe?\n\nWe'll use the notation st =[ c ]=> st' for the Com.EvalR relation:\nst =[ c ]=> st' means that executing program c in a starting state\nst results in an ending state st'.  This can be pronounced \"c takes\nstate st to st'\".\n\n\n\n","context":"Hoare Logic\u0009Imp: Simple Imperative Programs\u0009Evaluating Commands","header":"3.3.2. Evaluation as a Relation","id":"/Imp/#Hoare-Logic--Imp___-Simple-Imperative-Programs--Evaluating-Commands--Evaluation-as-a-Relation"}});